# 🧑‍💻 Developer guide

## 🏗️ Architecture

The generated plugin file is organized into 5 sections plus entrypoints:

~~text
scte_{{ plugin_name }}.py
├── 📥 Section 1: Imports and module constants
├── 🌐 Section 2: Global state
│   ├── PLUGIN_STATE, PLUGIN_STATE_LASTMOD (dormant unless OON preset revived)
│   ├── PENDING_CALLS (dormant unless OON preset revived)
│   └── AD_META_KEYS, API_URL, UAC_MODE (dormant unless OON preset revived)
├── ⚙️ Section 3: Config parsing helpers
│   ├── _parse_bool_config(), _parse_int_config(), _parse_float_config()
│   ├── call_sign(), api_url(), ad_meta_keys(), meta_key(), uac_mode()
│   └── Leak-safe scte_message summary builder
├── 📜 Section 4: Helpers used by log()
│   ├── HOST / PROC_ID discovery
│   ├── Frame introspection (caller filename + line number)
│   ├── Uppercase log level normalization
│   └── Redacted apikey copy for log output
├── 🎬 Section 5: Method implementations
{% for m in methods %}
│   ├── {{ m.name }}()  # channel_group={{ m.channel_group }}
{% endfor %}
│   └── (dispatched from Process35)
└── 🚪 Entrypoints
    ├── Process35(scte_message, ...)  # main SCTE dispatcher, loops methods
    └── HandleCall(...)                # async callback dispatcher
~~

🔁 The `Process35` dispatcher is loop-driven: it iterates registered methods
and calls whichever one matches the slicer's `channel_group`. Adding a
new preset means adding a new method function and registering it in the
methods loop — no dispatcher rewrite.

### 📞 Method contract

Every method (`{% for m in methods %}{{ m.name }}(){% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}`) receives the same argument shape from `Process35`:

- 📨 The parsed SCTE-35 message (as a dict-like object)
- 🕒 Slicer version, stream type, PTS, PTS offset, and other timing context
- ⚙️ Config-derived values (call sign, ID3 mode, etc.)

🔇 Return value is ignored — methods act via side effects (calling `slicer.*`
APIs to fire breaks, slates, or ID3 tags).

## 🔌 Extending the plugin

Two common extensions:

**➕ Add a new channel_group.** Copy an existing method (e.g. `{% if has_live_event %}{% for m in methods if m.preset == 'live_event' %}{{ m.name }}{% endfor %}{% else %}{% for m in methods %}{% if loop.first %}{{ m.name }}{% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endif %}`) in Section 5, rename it, adjust the segmentation
type_id branches to what your new customer sends, and register it in the
`Process35` methods loop. Then add a `channel_group: <new_name>` entry to
`uplynk.conf` on the slicer(s) that should use it.

**🔢 Handle a new SCTE-35 segmentation type_id.** Find the method for the
relevant `channel_group`, add an `elif type_id == 0x??:` branch, and
implement the action (slicer API call, log line, whatever).

💡 For non-trivial changes, regenerate the plugin from the source templates
rather than hand-editing — see the [scte-plugin-generator repo](https://github.com/jj358mhz/scte-plugin-generator).

